      "type": "function_call",
      "id": "fc_048f3780bc64831d016ad80e2df5348191a69cf2f3ad7bbb15",
      "call_id": "call_ee4b96fe88544622a9bb8f04deb57beb",
      "name": "bash",
      "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cat > /workspace/src/component.py <<'PY'\\ndef calculate_black_area_doubled(A: int, B: int, C: int, D: int) -> int:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return twice the black area in the specified wallpaper rectangle.\\n\\n    The arrangement is periodic but can cover very large coordinates, so the\\n    calculation is performed by counting unit squares rather than iterating\\n    over them.  In a unit square with lower-left corner ``(i, j)``, the\\n    diagonal ``x + y = even`` either runs through opposite corners or splits\\n    the square into two equal triangles.  In the latter case exactly half of\\n    the square is black, contributing one to the doubled area.\\n\\n    For squares where ``i + j`` is even, the square is monochromatic.  The\\n    black ones occur for ``i == 0 (mod 4)`` when ``j`` is even, and for\\n    ``i == 1 (mod 4)`` when ``j`` is odd.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n    def count_residue(lo: int, hi: int, residue: int, modulus: int) -> int:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Count integers n in [lo, hi) congruent to residue mod modulus.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        return ((hi - 1 - residue) // modulus\\n                - (lo - 1 - residue) // modulus)\\n\\n    i_even = count_residue(A, C, 0, 2)\\n    i_odd = count_residue(A, C, 1, 2)\\n    j_even = count_residue(B, D, 0, 2)\\n    j_odd = count_residue(B, D, 1, 2)\\n\\n    # Every such square is bisected by a diagonal and contributes 1 to\\n    # twice the area.\\n    diagonally_split = i_even * j_odd + i_odd * j_even\\n\\n    # The remaining even-parity squares are monochromatic. Count the black\\n    # ones by their period-four x-coordinate condition.\\n    black_monochromatic = (\\n        count_residue(A, C, 0, 4) * j_even\\n        + count_residue(A, C, 1, 4) * j_odd\\n    )\\n\\n    return diagonally_split + 2 * black_monochromatic\\nPY\\ncd /workspace && python tests.py\"}",
      "status": "completed"
    }
  ],
